    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im studying for my security+ and im stuck on one thing. if we are already hashing passwords, why do we even need to add a \"salt\" to them? wouldnt the hash alone be enough to keep it secure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Great question! While hashing passwords is essential for security, adding a salt to them enhances it further. Hashing alone can make your passwords vulnerable to rainbow table attacks, where precomputed tables of hash values are used to reverse-engineer passwords. Salts, being random and unique to each password, make it practically impossible for attackers to use these rainbow tables. Consequently, even if two users have the same password, they will have different salts, leading to entirely different hashes, thereby boosting security significantly.",
