    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so the salt just basically messes up the rainbow tables so they dont work anymore? can u give me a more specific example of how an attacker would use a rainbow table vs how the salt stops them? like walk me through the process.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! Let's imagine an attacker targets your database. Normally, without salts, if the password “P@ssw0rd!” was hashed, the attacker could quickly find the hash in a pre-made rainbow table and easily recover the plaintext password.\n\nNow, adding a salt changes things. Say the salt added to this password by your system is “xTgSj”! The attacker’s rainbow table is built without considering these unique salts. When the attacker obtains the salted hash, he doesn't find a matching entry in the precomputed table, as he can't anticipate the salt. Essentially, they now have to compute the hash of “P@ssw0rd!” with every possible salt in the highly impractical space – effectively thwarting the rainbow table attack.\n\nInstead of being able to match the hash to an existing plaintext password, the attacker would have to guess millions of possible salts and recompute the hash for each guess, which would be computationally expensive and time-consuming, often making it infeasible to achieve. By just adding a salt, even with a rainbow table, attackers gain no real advantage because they can't apply it to the stored, salted hashes—they underestimate the security layers added by the salts, making the attacks impractical.",
      "source": "target",
